[2026-11-13] rc:4568542751 inline on src/ipc/capnp/protocol.cpp by ryanofsky (member) (edited 2026-11-13):
In commit "ipc: hold event loop references when using it in CapnpProtocol" (2d001df8a60a82c286ca46b04de2917be4a5c74f)

There is a bug in the code I suggested: `done()` returns `m_num_refs == 0 && m_async_fns->empty()`, and `m_async_fns` is only set while `loop()` runs: it is [emplaced when loop() starts](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/blob/2d001df8a60a82c286ca46b04de2917be4a5c74f/src/ipc/libmultiprocess/src/mp/proxy.cpp#L298) and [reset before it returns](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/blob/2d001df8a60a82c286ca46b04de2917be4a5c74f/src/ipc/libmultiprocess/src/mp/proxy.cpp#L332). In `serve()`, after the parent disconnects and `loop()` returns, there is a window before `serve()` resets `m_loop` where a `makeStream()` or `disconnectIncoming()` call from another thread gets past the `!m_loop` check and calls `done()` with no references and `m_async_fns` unset. That is undefined behavior, and with `_GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS` it should abort.

This can be avoided by having `serve()` hold a reference to the loop, the way `startLoop()` does with `m_loop_ref`, and release it when the parent disconnects:

```cpp
auto [server, it] = mp::ServeStream<messages::Init>(*loop, make_stream(), init);
it->onDisconnect([this]() EXCLUSIVE_LOCKS_REQUIRED(!m_loop_mutex) {
    LOCK(m_loop_mutex);
    m_loop_ref.reset();
});
```

Then `loopRef()` can assume the loop is running and just add a reference:

```cpp
[[nodiscard]] std::optional<mp::EventLoopRef> loopRef() EXCLUSIVE_LOCKS_REQUIRED(!m_loop_mutex)
{
    LOCK(m_loop_mutex);
    if (!m_loop_ref) return std::nullopt;
    return mp::EventLoopRef{**m_loop_ref};
}
```

This drops the `done()` call and the try/catch in `disconnectIncoming()`, which becomes `if (!ref) return;`. It also drops `setup_ref`, which was only needed so `serve()` could call `loopRef()` before `loop()` started. `m_loop_ref` becomes `GUARDED_BY(m_loop_mutex)` and is set in the same locked block as `m_loop` in both `serve()` and `startLoop()`, and `connect()`, `listen()` and `makeStream()` throw "IPC event loop is not running" when `loopRef()` returns nullopt.

I tried this on top of 2d001df8, and `ipc_tests` and the IPC functional tests pass, including `rpc_misc.py`, whose `echoipc` spawns a child process that runs `serve()`. Full diff:

